        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Frequently asked questions</h2>

        {[
          { q: "How long should a UGC brief be?", a: "One to two pages maximum, in a scannable format with headers, bullet points, and bold text for key information. The creator should be able to read the entire brief in under 2 minutes and start planning immediately. If your brief is longer than two pages for a single deliverable, you are over-specifying. Use a Quick Reference section at the top with the absolute essentials: product name, deliverable count, aspect ratio, duration, and deadline." },
          { q: "Should I script the entire video for the creator?", a: "No. Give structure, not scripts. Specify the hook framework, key messages to hit, and the CTA, but let the creator deliver everything in their own words and tone. Over-scripted UGC sounds forced and performs worse than authentic, creator-driven content. The entire value proposition of UGC is that it feels real. If you want word-for-word control, hire an actor, not a UGC creator." },
          { q: "What file format should I deliver the brief in?", a: "Google Doc or Notion page, not PDF. Editable formats let you update the brief in real time if details change, and the creator always sees the latest version. PDFs become outdated the moment you update a detail, and you have no way of knowing which version the creator downloaded. For high-volume programs, a shared Notion workspace with a template database is the most efficient setup." },
          { q: "How many hook variations should I request per video?", a: "Three to five hook variations per concept. This gives your media buying team enough options to test in paid media without overwhelming the creator. Each hook variation should use a different framework (question, bold claim, problem-agitation) or angle. Hooks are the highest-leverage variable in ad performance, so testing multiple hooks per video is the fastest path to finding winners." },
          { q: "Can I reuse the same brief for different products?", a: "Yes, with a master template approach. Create a master brief template that covers your brand overview, target audience, general dos and don'ts, and usage rights. Then create product-specific modules that slot into the template with product details, key claims, and unique selling points. This lets you brief new products in 10 to 15 minutes instead of starting from scratch each time." },
        ].map((item) => (
